WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.860
Today's show is sponsored by
Arnold Schwarzenegger's One-Sided Chess Set.

00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:05.600
'I'll be black.'

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:10.540
This is the Technical Difficulties.
We're playing Citation Needed.

00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:12.760
I have an almost-randomly selected article

00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:15.220
from everybody's favourite
reliable source of knowledge, Wikipedia,

00:00:15.220 --> 00:00:17.200
and these folks can't see it.

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:19.620
Every fact they get right is a point and a ding [DING],

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:22.120
and there's a special prize
for particularly good answers, which is...

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:28.140
And today we have an article with 'multiple issues'.

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:30.020
ALL: Oooo.

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:32.060
It needs additional citations;

00:00:32.060 --> 00:00:34.200
it may need to be rewritten entirely...

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:38.180
But nevertheless, we are talking about the flag of Mars.

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:40.660
Right.

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:43.000
So this is a question about the people
who make Galaxy chocolate, yes?

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:44.280
Yes, totally, yes.

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:47.580
It's a bit like a Japanese flag,
only it's a big Malteser in the middle.

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:51.340
Surely that's the Maltese flag?

00:00:51.740 --> 00:00:53.580
Ohhh, you!

00:00:53.740 --> 00:00:54.680
Oh, he's got you there!

00:00:54.820 --> 00:00:56.340
He got me bang to rights!

00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:01.280
Meanwhile, across the river...
there is the flag of Cadbury's.

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:03.180
Yes.
[Laughter]

00:01:03.340 --> 00:01:07.580
'We march beneath our purple banner to GLORY!'

00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:10.120
Just loading cannons, again with Maltesers.

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:12.220
Just as grapeshot.

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:15.680
'This is the most delicious battle ev —
AAUGH!'

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:16.760
Aaah!

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:19.340
'Still pretty good...'

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:22.980
Honeycomb and chocolate in the eye at velocity!

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:25.980
So goggles, obviously, is the first consideration,
I think we can all agree.

00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:28.660
Hershey's won't join until 1941.

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:29.540
ALL: Ohhh!
[Laughter]

00:01:29.660 --> 00:01:30.400
Yes!

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:33.600
And it'll just be terrible anyway.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:35.320
That's more of a comment on their chocolate.

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:38.280
Cadbury's sending in the 1st Mounted Freddos,
as the first....

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:41.820
No, they'd send in Taz's!
Could you imagine the devastation?

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:45.240
That's why Freddos went up to 20p.
There was a shortage after the battle.

00:01:45.340 --> 00:01:46.520
Right. Massacred. Massacred.

00:01:46.660 --> 00:01:49.460
Caramel guts sprayed all over the place!

00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:52.000
'Leave me! Leave me! Go on yourself!'

00:01:52.180 --> 00:01:53.120
'Aaargh!'

00:01:56.860 --> 00:01:58.940
I've just got the thought of Mars...

00:01:58.940 --> 00:02:01.160
like they were multiple rocket-launchers in World War II,

00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:02.560
a Mars bar, like that.

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:04.360
Thump! Thump! Thump!

00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:06.880
I've just got the idea that Swiss chocolate
is sitting in the middle.

00:02:07.720 --> 00:02:08.800
— 'Not happening!'
— Nope, nope.

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:11.220
Curly Wurlys used as trenching ladders.

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:12.300
[Laughter]

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:14.860
Using Revels as grenades.

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:16.700
Do you remember the Cadbury's Caramel rabbit?

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:18.860
— Yes I do.
— She'd be Florence Nightingale.

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:22.440
I was thinking she was the cheesecake for the troops.

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:25.140
— Oh crikey. Steady lad!
— And the old ENSA shows.

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:26.440
Be still!

00:02:27.020 --> 00:02:30.080
[Husky voice]
'Hi boys... I'll just undo the wrapper slightly...'

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:31.540
[Cheers]

00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:34.780
'Show us your gooey centre!'

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:36.720
[Laughter]
[Groans]

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:42.800
That's the greatest laugh I've ever heard!

00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:47.140
Don't make him self-conscious of his laugh.
He's got to keep it going.

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:48.960
No! That would be even worse.

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:50.360
It was actually — you know what...

00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:53.580
Horribly, I was laughing over the thought
of one-legged Freddos...

00:02:57.240 --> 00:03:00.960
'Yeah, lost the leg back in '15 in the Chocolate Wars.'

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:04.300
'They did melt a new one onto me, but it's not the same.'

00:03:04.520 --> 00:03:06.520
It's a leg from a Santa.

00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:11.740
I just got the thought of Taz's
riding those Lindt rabbits.

00:03:12.380 --> 00:03:14.380
No, Lindt... Oh, no, Lindt's German, isn't it.

00:03:14.380 --> 00:03:16.920
It's not Swiss. I thought, yeah, Swiss neutrality, but yeah.

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:19.480
'Chaaarge!'

00:03:19.700 --> 00:03:22.240
Christ, have I told you about this,
when I went to the Lindt factory...

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:23.560
— Hello!
— ...in Germany.

00:03:23.740 --> 00:03:25.560
Did you find the weapons stockpile or something?

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:27.940
No, sadly it's even better than that.

00:03:27.940 --> 00:03:30.120
Because... you know, you go to the Cadbury's thing,

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:32.420
and it's pretty much on the site of the real factory...

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:34.380
— Yeah.
— You're just one room over.

00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:38.020
Well, Lindt have got this little one
that's on an island in one of the rivers...

00:03:38.020 --> 00:03:40.300
I forget which German city it is, I do apologize.

00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:42.760
And so you go on through,
and they've got the fake little factory bit,

00:03:42.760 --> 00:03:45.160
and then there's this big display board about Mr Lindt.

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:46.060
Okay.

00:03:46.220 --> 00:03:50.480
Saying, 'da da da... Mr Lindt started this company
in blah blah blah blah in da da da...'

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:54.460
Then the war starts: 'Sadly, Mr Lindt was tragically killed in 1918.'

00:03:54.460 --> 00:03:58.100
So it's like, oh, so he went to war, you know...
it was a bombing raid or whatever...

00:03:58.280 --> 00:04:02.460
No! Mr Lindt died when one of his own
fondant boilers exploded.

00:04:02.620 --> 00:04:03.600
Ohhh!

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:05.580
And covered him in delicious goo!

00:04:05.780 --> 00:04:07.680
— If you're gonna go...!
— Death by chocolate!

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:08.800
[Laughter]

00:04:09.740 --> 00:04:13.380
And if someone didn't say that at the time...
[Laughter]

00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:16.840
'Eh? I know it's a little bit soon but, er...'

00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:19.760
[Together]  'Death by chocolate? Eh?'

00:04:19.980 --> 00:04:22.420
I can hear the damn muted trombone...

00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:24.360
ALL: Womp womp womp...

00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:27.300
'No, it was horrifically heated caramel, Vicar.'

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:29.120
[Laughter]

00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:31.820
'There's a reason the casket is closed.'

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:34.640
'But, er...
Still good!'

00:04:36.440 --> 00:04:39.840
'In death, as in life... Mr Lindt was delicious.'

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:41.520
[Laughter]

00:04:42.700 --> 00:04:46.860
'All right [??], he may now be just
the human equivalent of a Daim bar...'

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:51.240
Oh! Bad time to take a sip of water, Matt.

00:04:52.540 --> 00:04:55.980
'Soft on the outside, crunchy on the inside — Mr Lindt!'

00:04:59.780 --> 00:05:03.460
I almost feel sorry for a man who's been dead
nearly a hundred years now.

00:05:08.260 --> 00:05:09.420
A long time ago...

00:05:09.500 --> 00:05:11.500
ALL: In a galaxy far, far away...

00:05:11.640 --> 00:05:12.620
Galaxy!

00:05:12.760 --> 00:05:14.520
ALL: Heyyy!

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:16.800
We were talking about the flag of Mars.

00:05:16.920 --> 00:05:18.620
Yes.
Is it red?

00:05:19.220 --> 00:05:20.500
Only in part.

00:05:20.920 --> 00:05:22.120
Is the red bit red?

00:05:22.200 --> 00:05:23.520
— Yes.
— Ah, there we go.

00:05:23.620 --> 00:05:24.980
— Point. [DING]
— Yaaay!

00:05:25.640 --> 00:05:27.060
Not sure why.

00:05:27.220 --> 00:05:30.400
Is the red bit Mars-shaped, i.e. a circle?

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:33.360
No, not at all. It's very much symbolic, and...

00:05:33.540 --> 00:05:36.700
Is it a red horizon and then
sort of a black upper band or something?

00:05:36.860 --> 00:05:38.300
Ooh, close to that.

00:05:38.440 --> 00:05:41.200
— Blue upper band?
— Oh, with two stars, the Earth and the Moon?

00:05:41.800 --> 00:05:42.980
— And the sun?
— No...

00:05:43.060 --> 00:05:45.060
I'm going to give you a point for blue band as well. [DING]

00:05:45.060 --> 00:05:46.480
You've got a red band and a blue band...

00:05:46.600 --> 00:05:47.280
Oh, okay.

00:05:47.420 --> 00:05:50.140
It's a tricolor, like the French flag or the German flag.

00:05:50.300 --> 00:05:51.560
Right, okay — what, sideways?

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:52.660
Sideways.

00:05:53.460 --> 00:05:54.260
Red, blue...

00:05:54.360 --> 00:05:57.180
Red, blue and green,
after Kim Stanley Robinson's Mars Trilogy.

00:05:57.360 --> 00:05:59.000
[DING]
No way!

00:05:59.220 --> 00:06:00.740
No way!

00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:04.220
Red Mars, Green Mars, Blue Mars, in that order,

00:06:04.220 --> 00:06:06.700
representing past and possible futures of Mars.

00:06:07.080 --> 00:06:09.080
Oh, right, green being when we astroturf it.

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:10.640
...manage to terraform it.

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:12.120
And blue when it has water. Yes.

00:06:12.280 --> 00:06:15.040
Right. So the green is the bit where we turf it over

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:17.400
and have a country park or something like that —
a picnic area...

00:06:17.560 --> 00:06:19.880
Just —
that's where we're going to send all the football.

00:06:19.880 --> 00:06:21.620
Because we don't need it here on Earth.

00:06:22.160 --> 00:06:23.260
I would disagree.

00:06:23.420 --> 00:06:24.960
That is a great, great idea.

00:06:25.820 --> 00:06:26.660
...No.

00:06:26.860 --> 00:06:28.860
You don't have to agree.
You're welcome to go to Mars too!

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:32.600
No, no, I will be on the supporters' bus
to Mars and back on a Saturday.

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:34.800
I just like the idea of low-gravity football now.

00:06:35.440 --> 00:06:37.240
Mmm, that's got a certain charm to it, hasn't it.

00:06:37.420 --> 00:06:38.720
It'd be a sod to control.

00:06:38.920 --> 00:06:40.600
You hoof it, and it comes back and hits you in the head.

00:06:40.700 --> 00:06:42.900
Well, did you ever play Brazilian football at school,

00:06:42.900 --> 00:06:44.920
where you're in the hall,
and off the walls is perfectly valid?

00:06:45.080 --> 00:06:46.620
— Yes, I did, yes, yes...
— I never played that!

00:06:46.780 --> 00:06:50.240
Ah, it's great. It's a game of football
I can actually get behind.

00:06:50.740 --> 00:06:53.620
Imagine that — if we could get one of these floaty...

00:06:53.620 --> 00:06:55.620
You know the big fan chambers
where you pretend you're skydiving?

00:06:55.780 --> 00:06:56.560
[Gasps]

00:06:56.700 --> 00:06:58.100
— Ohhh!
— Oh no, you see, I've been to one of them.

00:06:58.180 --> 00:06:59.860
You can't control anything in it.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:01.000
Your bladder?

00:07:04.280 --> 00:07:06.600
Yes. You have to wear a diaper before you go in...

00:07:06.700 --> 00:07:08.700
Tom Scott's Anti-Gravity S***ting.

00:07:08.780 --> 00:07:09.840
Nappy, surely.

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:11.620
Tom Scott: Pissing In The Wind.

00:07:17.020 --> 00:07:18.640
'Aaaaah!'

00:07:19.060 --> 00:07:21.580
[Together]  'I'm singing in the rain...'

00:07:21.760 --> 00:07:24.000
Ohh! You horrible human beings.

00:07:24.940 --> 00:07:26.940
So what's relative gravity on Mars?

00:07:27.700 --> 00:07:29.560
— Er...
— Give me a moment...

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:31.580
— More than the Moon.
— More than a six. It's nearly one-to-one, isn't it?

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:35.420
It's actually about a third of gravity — 0.37.

00:07:35.420 --> 00:07:36.260
— Really?
— Yeah.

00:07:36.340 --> 00:07:37.640
What, less — is it a third...?

00:07:37.760 --> 00:07:40.740
Okay. So I'm going to grasp sketchily here
with my poor knowledge of physics...

00:07:40.740 --> 00:07:43.340
but are we now talking about a pitch
that's three times the size

00:07:43.340 --> 00:07:44.580
for the ball to come down?

00:07:44.580 --> 00:07:46.780
Or do we just make the ball three times heavier?

00:07:46.940 --> 00:07:50.160
I just think you ask players
to control themselves, quite frankly.

00:07:50.300 --> 00:07:52.560
Oh, you make it a game of subtlety and finesse

00:07:52.680 --> 00:07:54.520
Yes, and learn the Martian game.

00:07:54.640 --> 00:07:56.340
— Oooh.
— Oooh!

00:07:56.520 --> 00:07:59.120
You can tell who actually bothered by football, here.

00:07:59.200 --> 00:08:01.120
Because you'd be able to jump higher...

00:08:01.200 --> 00:08:02.320
— Ohhh!
— So, theoretically...

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:04.080
Headers'd be marvelous!

00:08:04.220 --> 00:08:05.100
— I think...
— It's not...

00:08:05.420 --> 00:08:08.380
I think with a third gravity you could expect someone

00:08:08.380 --> 00:08:10.160
to be able to jump over someone else.

00:08:10.300 --> 00:08:11.320
— Yeah.
— I don't think that's an unreasonable...

00:08:11.460 --> 00:08:12.480
It'd be like Quidditch!

00:08:13.060 --> 00:08:15.940
I'm just thinking, actually,
we take it back to the futsal thing,

00:08:15.940 --> 00:08:18.200
and off the ceiling is perfectly valid.

00:08:18.620 --> 00:08:20.300
Oh, you would have to have a ceiling, you're right.

00:08:20.300 --> 00:08:21.440
You would have to have a roof, you would.

00:08:21.540 --> 00:08:22.660
That solves it, yeah.

00:08:22.740 --> 00:08:23.740
It would have to be indoors, and therefore...

00:08:23.880 --> 00:08:26.240
Because otherwise you're going to be running for miles
to get the damn ball back.

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:28.160
Yeah. And a third gravity...

00:08:28.400 --> 00:08:32.900
Potentially, I mean, how long could you do
the halfway wall-running thing?

00:08:32.980 --> 00:08:34.980
— Ohhh.
— Under those auspices?

00:08:35.100 --> 00:08:39.300
'I'm just gonna dribble it three feet up
on the wall here past you...' You know.

00:08:39.460 --> 00:08:42.060
I wouldn't, no. I wouldn't allow that.
I think you'd... no.

00:08:42.060 --> 00:08:44.720
I wouldn't be fine with running up the walls.

00:08:44.920 --> 00:08:46.920
— Well, you're not, you're running along the wall.
— What if you had, like, curved...

00:08:46.920 --> 00:08:48.460
Oh, yeah!

00:08:48.680 --> 00:08:49.800
Like a velodrome.

00:08:50.020 --> 00:08:51.900
Like a half-pipe. A big half-pipe.

00:08:52.020 --> 00:08:54.840
Because you'd be able to get enough speed up
to do these curving runs...

00:08:55.000 --> 00:08:57.100
Nah, it's not the spirit.
I think people would just have to accept that...

00:08:57.180 --> 00:08:58.600
— Oh, yeah! Because the spirit of the game...
— Not in the spirit of the game!

00:08:58.740 --> 00:09:01.200
The spirit of the game is on Mars.

00:09:01.340 --> 00:09:02.620
Is on Mars. Yes.

00:09:02.760 --> 00:09:05.040
Well, I think that Mars City,
as they will undoubtedly be known...

00:09:05.040 --> 00:09:08.260
or United, there may be two teams.
It may be able to support two teams, I don't know...

00:09:08.260 --> 00:09:10.260
would inevitably have a home advantage.

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:11.240
The Mars Derby!

00:09:11.380 --> 00:09:12.720
The Mars Derby would be the...

00:09:12.840 --> 00:09:14.140
No, they wouldn't have home advantage.

00:09:14.140 --> 00:09:15.900
Don't forget that the original premise is,

00:09:15.900 --> 00:09:18.380
we're shipping every one of the buggers up there!

00:09:18.520 --> 00:09:21.160
Oh no, I was just thinking of starting a...
of having a team up there and...

00:09:21.320 --> 00:09:23.260
Nope! Ship 'em all off. Goodbye!

00:09:23.360 --> 00:09:24.700
One hell of an away trip.

00:09:24.840 --> 00:09:27.220
It'd be a bit like when QPR had astroturfed the pitch,

00:09:27.220 --> 00:09:28.460
everyone said they had an unfair advantage.

00:09:28.460 --> 00:09:30.100
I think it'd be the same with Mars United.

00:09:30.100 --> 00:09:31.680
I think they would have an unfair advantage.

00:09:31.840 --> 00:09:33.300
Because they'd have all the breathing apparatus.

00:09:33.420 --> 00:09:34.720
GARY: They would. They would.

00:09:35.800 --> 00:09:37.800
Like Arnie in Total Recall.

00:09:38.080 --> 00:09:40.680
I tell you what, the away attendances
would be shocking, wouldn't they.

00:09:41.120 --> 00:09:41.840
When you're a few years...

00:09:42.020 --> 00:09:43.980
'And it's a very lifeless crowd here today...'
[Laughter]

00:09:45.540 --> 00:09:48.740
When you're a few years down the line, though,
we'd have people who'd grown up on Mars,

00:09:48.740 --> 00:09:51.440
who are presumably taller, more adapted to gravity.

00:09:51.960 --> 00:09:53.500
— Like, at that point...
— They couldn't transfer to the Earth game, could they.

00:09:53.600 --> 00:09:54.700
— They wouldn't have the muscle tone.
— No.

00:09:54.800 --> 00:09:57.840
They'd look at us as a race of
semi-evolved dwarfs, wouldn't they?

00:09:57.980 --> 00:09:59.980
Which is why I think we should just nuke Mars now.

00:10:00.380 --> 00:10:02.380
And just nip this in the bud.

00:10:02.380 --> 00:10:03.840
Get the first dibs in.

00:10:04.040 --> 00:10:06.360
What you're saying is, we should just
fire a Mars at Mars now.

00:10:06.480 --> 00:10:08.440
Yes. I think we really should.

00:10:08.440 --> 00:10:10.440
And leave the Freddos where they are.

00:10:10.440 --> 00:10:11.960
They've suffered enough.

00:10:12.140 --> 00:10:15.060
'Eat nougaty death, ye Martian bastards!'

00:10:15.360 --> 00:10:18.860
I'm just thinking...
because there's the idea of 'Rods from God'...

00:10:19.120 --> 00:10:21.680
Yes! Hyper-accelerated Mars bar.

00:10:21.820 --> 00:10:23.180
— Boomf.
— Yeah! Whoomp!

00:10:23.320 --> 00:10:24.620
Rods from God — do you know the concept?

00:10:24.620 --> 00:10:27.040
The idea that the only space weapon you really need

00:10:27.040 --> 00:10:30.760
is a big titanium rod just sitting on a satellite,

00:10:31.040 --> 00:10:33.980
and then when you're over the city you want,
you just drop it.

00:10:34.220 --> 00:10:36.300
Poor Titanium Rod, sat up there on his own.

00:10:36.460 --> 00:10:38.360
I'd like to see if you could get, like,
Rod Stewart and drop him on...

00:10:38.500 --> 00:10:39.240
— Yes.
— Ha!

00:10:39.360 --> 00:10:41.080
[Sings]  'Baaaby Jane...'

00:10:41.300 --> 00:10:42.260
No, that's more...

00:10:43.520 --> 00:10:46.820
That's more psychological warfare.
You parachute him in, still alive.

00:10:47.600 --> 00:10:50.440
'My name is Titanium Rod.'

00:10:50.440 --> 00:10:54.600
'And I, ironically, am the man
who's in charge of the titanium rods.'

00:10:55.440 --> 00:10:58.260
When it arrives it has as much kinetic energy
as an atomic bomb would.

00:10:58.560 --> 00:11:00.560
And it levels the city.

00:11:00.560 --> 00:11:02.620
And all you need to do is
get the thing up there in the first place.

00:11:03.560 --> 00:11:04.940
— How would you do that?
— 'All you need to do,' yeah.

00:11:05.100 --> 00:11:07.560
*Someone* will drop one, won't they,
while they're up there.

00:11:07.640 --> 00:11:08.620
'Ooop! Bugger.'

00:11:08.740 --> 00:11:10.120
— Ohhh...
— 'Oh, there she goes...'

00:11:10.260 --> 00:11:11.020
'Oh, s***.'

00:11:11.160 --> 00:11:14.840
'Hello? Is this Chicago?
I've, er, got some bad news...'

00:11:14.960 --> 00:11:16.780
There's actually a thing that would prevent both...

00:11:16.780 --> 00:11:20.000
You know, a legal thing that prevents both
the development of weapons like that,

00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:22.560
*and* it's the reason the flag of Mars isn't official.

00:11:22.560 --> 00:11:23.900
Does anyone know what it is?

00:11:24.060 --> 00:11:25.360
Who owns Mars?

00:11:25.480 --> 00:11:27.640
Bet they've sold off plots of it
like they have on the Moon.

00:11:27.820 --> 00:11:28.500
Yeah.

00:11:28.640 --> 00:11:31.560
They absolutely have, but again,
this is the thing that prevents it.

00:11:31.680 --> 00:11:33.680
— Does anyone know what the...?
— No.

00:11:33.840 --> 00:11:36.160
The International Treaty on
Not Being a Dick About Mars.

00:11:36.280 --> 00:11:38.100
I'm going to give you a point.
It's the Outer Space Treaty.

00:11:38.200 --> 00:11:38.800
Yeah, Interstellar Treaty...

00:11:38.940 --> 00:11:39.720
[DING]
Yeah.

00:11:40.080 --> 00:11:43.040
The Outer Space Treaty
bans weapons of mass destruction

00:11:43.040 --> 00:11:45.320
in orbit, or on the Moon, or anywhere else.

00:11:45.600 --> 00:11:48.900
And it also means that all the celestial bodies
are open to mankind.

00:11:49.040 --> 00:11:50.320
Did anyone tell China about that?

00:11:50.440 --> 00:11:53.220
Yeah, it'll presumably last
until someone actually lands there.

00:11:53.340 --> 00:11:55.140
— Yeah. 'Now it's ours.'
— At which point... yes.

00:11:55.820 --> 00:11:59.580
Could you take a medical titanium rod
into space under that pretense?

00:11:59.740 --> 00:12:01.540
That's what happened with the supergun, isn't it?

00:12:01.540 --> 00:12:03.060
— Yeah, it was.
— The supergun?

00:12:03.200 --> 00:12:06.780
After the ban on NBC for Iraq...

00:12:06.940 --> 00:12:08.720
Nuclear, Biological, Chemical, yeah.

00:12:08.880 --> 00:12:13.120
After the ban for that in Iraq,
he started building an enormous...

00:12:13.120 --> 00:12:13.780
Er, 'he'..

00:12:13.780 --> 00:12:16.860
Saddam commissioned
an enormous conventional gun,

00:12:16.860 --> 00:12:19.100
with a bore of about a metre, I think.

00:12:19.300 --> 00:12:22.300
I think the basic thought was,
'If I can't have biological and chemical weapons,

00:12:22.300 --> 00:12:24.440
'I'll make the biggest, f***-off-est gun I can...'

00:12:24.640 --> 00:12:26.220
Yeah. They've got a piece in...

00:12:26.420 --> 00:12:28.180
Project Babylon.

00:12:28.300 --> 00:12:29.880
— Oh cool!
— Heya.

00:12:30.080 --> 00:12:32.080
[Matt] It fires gardens!
[Laughter]

00:12:32.640 --> 00:12:35.380
The whole thing sort of fell apart, and whatever else,

00:12:35.500 --> 00:12:39.220
but they've got a section of the barrel
in Duxford Air Museum.

00:12:39.220 --> 00:12:40.320
I think it was Duxford — anyway...

00:12:40.540 --> 00:12:42.540
'...barrel length of 46 metres'.

00:12:42.680 --> 00:12:44.840
— Aww yeah.
— And that was the baby one, the first one.

00:12:44.940 --> 00:12:47.880
I'm looking around, looking at it...
'Made in Sheffield'.

00:12:48.020 --> 00:12:48.640
Yeah.

00:12:48.880 --> 00:12:50.600
No way!
[Laughter]

00:12:51.260 --> 00:12:53.860
It was a company called Forgemasters, if I don't forget...

00:12:54.020 --> 00:12:55.840
— Yeah.
— If I remember rightly, that were...

00:12:56.000 --> 00:12:59.300
It does say here, 'The metal tubes...
were purchased from firms in the United Kingdom,

00:12:59.300 --> 00:13:01.660
'...including Sheffield Forgemasters.'

00:13:01.780 --> 00:13:03.780
That's it. And if you're going to have a steel company,

00:13:03.780 --> 00:13:05.560
you call it something like that.

00:13:05.800 --> 00:13:07.640
— Sheffield Forgemasters, yeah.
— Forgemasters!

00:13:07.900 --> 00:13:10.360
Well, basically, they were told it was drainage pipe

00:13:10.360 --> 00:13:13.680
that they were making,
*with a bore on it* in the middle.

00:13:13.820 --> 00:13:18.140
'Yeah, we like to rifle our s***
so it gets away from the cities faster.'

00:13:18.260 --> 00:13:19.500
[Laughter]

00:13:19.680 --> 00:13:21.860
Just get right in the groove there!
Ptoom... Whoof!

00:13:22.700 --> 00:13:24.700
'Were there any bullets left in the gun?'

00:13:24.700 --> 00:13:26.940
'No, sir. By the time we got there, they'd been rifled.'

00:13:27.080 --> 00:13:29.080
[Laughter and groans]

00:13:29.380 --> 00:13:31.680
Yeah, Big Babylon was meant to be a one...

00:13:32.180 --> 00:13:34.180
Ha! Sorry — 'Big Babylon'!

00:13:34.300 --> 00:13:36.300
...a one-metre-bore supergun.

00:13:36.300 --> 00:13:38.720
A space gun. It would shoot into...

00:13:38.880 --> 00:13:40.700
Oh, low orbit and then drop back down, yeah.

00:13:40.800 --> 00:13:42.220
I seem to remember they got...

00:13:42.220 --> 00:13:45.780
It was an MP or someone,
the guy that basically rumbled the whole thing.

00:13:45.860 --> 00:13:46.580
Yeah.

00:13:46.580 --> 00:13:47.520
Didn't he come up with the great phrase of,

00:13:47.520 --> 00:13:51.380
'They said it was a sewage pipe, but how come it had
a thumping great muzzle on the end?'

00:13:52.080 --> 00:13:54.080
Well, there's some lovely commentary here, that...

00:13:54.080 --> 00:13:59.640
'Neither of these devices could be elevated or trained,
making them useless for direct military purposes.'

00:13:59.640 --> 00:14:01.060
You couldn't aim them.

00:14:01.220 --> 00:14:02.960
— You just shot at *something*.
— Yes.

00:14:03.300 --> 00:14:05.300
I think you vaguely aim at something in the distance,

00:14:05.300 --> 00:14:07.540
and whatever it hits on, you go,
'See? We could move it...'

00:14:07.680 --> 00:14:09.680
And it's also immobile,

00:14:09.680 --> 00:14:12.260
which means it had the same
problem as Germany's V-3:

00:14:12.260 --> 00:14:14.040
You can bomb it.

00:14:14.040 --> 00:14:15.440
It's there.

00:14:16.340 --> 00:14:18.340
It's five hundred feet long.

00:14:18.500 --> 00:14:20.020
You're not going to miss that, are you?

00:14:20.120 --> 00:14:21.160
You're not going to miss that.

00:14:21.300 --> 00:14:22.500
You can see it from Mars.

00:14:22.660 --> 00:14:24.660
I'll just fill the barrel with chocolate and watch it explode.

00:14:24.840 --> 00:14:26.840
'Right, we've filled it with fondant, lads...'

00:14:26.960 --> 00:14:30.080
'Next time it goes off,
it's Mr Lindt all over again!'

00:14:30.860 --> 00:14:34.720
There is somewhere on Earth
where the Mars flag is flying right now.

00:14:35.000 --> 00:14:37.000
Kim Stanley Robinson's back yard.

00:14:37.240 --> 00:14:39.780
Somewhere it's officially flying, by governmental decree.

00:14:39.940 --> 00:14:42.600
Is it the same as another country's flag?

00:14:42.780 --> 00:14:44.220
— No, not at all.
— Okay.

00:14:44.220 --> 00:14:46.080
I'm going to get bone obvious.

00:14:46.080 --> 00:14:47.920
Are we saying something like Nasa

00:14:47.920 --> 00:14:49.920
or the European Space Agency or something like that?

00:14:50.080 --> 00:14:51.420
It's one of their facilities, yes.

00:14:51.420 --> 00:14:54.660
The Flashline Mars Arctic Research Station —
which is a great name.

00:14:54.820 --> 00:14:55.840
— Yes!
— Oh, okay.

00:14:55.960 --> 00:14:57.960
Does anyone want to take a guess where that is?

00:14:58.100 --> 00:14:59.460
— In the Arctic.
— Is it in the Arctic?

00:14:59.580 --> 00:15:01.440
Point. [DING] Absolutely.

00:15:01.580 --> 00:15:03.540
[Laughter]
It's not a guess. You told us the answer.

00:15:03.660 --> 00:15:05.360
Oh.
It's in the name!

00:15:05.580 --> 00:15:08.460
Is the Flashline Arctic Research Station in the Arctic?

00:15:08.460 --> 00:15:10.380
No! It's in Venezuela.

00:15:10.700 --> 00:15:12.700
I just read the name!

00:15:12.700 --> 00:15:14.700
I just... I read the name. I didn't...
[Laughter]

00:15:14.700 --> 00:15:16.340
...didn't notice...

00:15:16.580 --> 00:15:17.800
I thought that was intentional!

00:15:17.940 --> 00:15:19.220
Keeping the point though!

00:15:19.340 --> 00:15:21.400
There have been a couple of other fictional Mars flags.

00:15:21.400 --> 00:15:23.620
This is the one that's sort of vaguely acceptable

00:15:23.620 --> 00:15:25.600
Does anyone want to take a guess
what they might have been,

00:15:25.600 --> 00:15:26.420
and where they might have been?

00:15:26.600 --> 00:15:28.600
Red background and blue letters: 'Mars'.

00:15:29.620 --> 00:15:31.620
You know what, you are so close.

00:15:31.760 --> 00:15:32.560
[Laughter]
Oh, okay.

00:15:32.680 --> 00:15:33.840
— No way.
— It's not actually...

00:15:34.000 --> 00:15:37.720
It's red lettering... it's red on a white background.

00:15:37.840 --> 00:15:38.500
Yeah?

00:15:38.660 --> 00:15:41.100
And it's not quite 'Mars' but it's something very similar.

00:15:41.320 --> 00:15:43.320
This is from Heinlein's Stranger in a Strange Land.

00:15:43.440 --> 00:15:45.640
I've not read that one.
I've read some Heinlein but not that one.

00:15:45.820 --> 00:15:47.700
It was simply the symbol for Mars:

00:15:47.700 --> 00:15:49.700
the male symbol, the circle with an arrow.

00:15:49.860 --> 00:15:51.340
— Ahhh.
— Ohh.

00:15:51.540 --> 00:15:55.260
The side effect of that being, you are essentially
just planting a sign that says, 'MEN!'

00:15:56.080 --> 00:15:58.400
What a surprise! From Heinlein!

00:15:58.540 --> 00:16:00.060
Er, yes, good point.

00:16:00.200 --> 00:16:03.940
Basically you'd be planting a flag that's got
Austin Powers' medallion on it.

00:16:04.040 --> 00:16:05.440
Yes. Mojo symbol.

00:16:05.640 --> 00:16:07.620
And meanwhile on Venus,

00:16:07.620 --> 00:16:10.200
there is a flag that is just literally...

00:16:10.420 --> 00:16:12.060
There's a flag that's on fire.

00:16:12.060 --> 00:16:14.060
Briefly, before it's destroyed.

00:16:14.220 --> 00:16:16.700
Well, you say that...
There was a plan that someone came up with

00:16:16.700 --> 00:16:18.960
to colonize the upper atmosphere of Venus.

00:16:19.080 --> 00:16:20.360
So what, fully orbital?

00:16:20.440 --> 00:16:21.860
Everyone just has to tread water.

00:16:22.200 --> 00:16:25.820
No, because the upper atmosphere
is about atmospheric pressure.

00:16:26.040 --> 00:16:30.480
It's toxic air, but if it's at atmospheric pressure
and you get a leak,

00:16:30.640 --> 00:16:33.120
'Oh. I will walk up and I will seal the leak.'

00:16:33.220 --> 00:16:34.500
Put some Blu-Tack in it.

00:16:34.660 --> 00:16:37.740
The temperature's about habitable,
the pressure's about habitable.

00:16:37.740 --> 00:16:40.460
So if you can keep a giant balloon up there,

00:16:40.460 --> 00:16:42.760
and the atmosphere is dense enough
that you could do that,

00:16:42.760 --> 00:16:44.460
because our air is lighter,

00:16:44.460 --> 00:16:46.880
you could theoretically have a bubble, just sitting...

00:16:47.040 --> 00:16:48.580
Just a bubble city...

00:16:48.760 --> 00:16:49.600
Yeah.

00:16:49.700 --> 00:16:50.520
Cool!

00:16:50.660 --> 00:16:53.880
Mind you, that titanium rod program
is coming on a treat.

00:16:54.140 --> 00:16:56.440
Actually, going back to the earlier part of this conversation,

00:16:56.440 --> 00:16:58.580
could you get a chocolate bar that could do that?

00:16:58.940 --> 00:17:02.280
I think reentry might pose a problem for a Mars bar.

00:17:02.380 --> 00:17:03.420
What if it were in a wrapper?

00:17:04.240 --> 00:17:06.160
— What, like Eminem?
— Yeah. [Laughs]

00:17:06.320 --> 00:17:08.060
So what you're espousing is,

00:17:08.060 --> 00:17:12.900
putting a Mars bar in like, a round
that will burn up perfectly through reentry...

00:17:13.040 --> 00:17:14.180
GARY: Yeeeup.

00:17:14.300 --> 00:17:17.060
Leaving you with a solid Mars bar,
which will then refreeze in the upper atmosphere...

00:17:17.180 --> 00:17:17.940
Correct.

00:17:18.100 --> 00:17:19.620
Slowly defrost slightly, and then...

00:17:19.760 --> 00:17:22.180
Well, it's not going to be as damaging,
but I like the way you're thinking.

00:17:22.280 --> 00:17:22.960
Yeah.

00:17:23.120 --> 00:17:25.120
That's the ultimate middle finger, isn't it?

00:17:25.280 --> 00:17:27.900
[American accent] 'Well, I do believe we are
gonna attack you with nuclear weapons.'

00:17:28.140 --> 00:17:29.420
'Ooh! Mars bar!'

00:17:29.560 --> 00:17:31.740
'Nuclear weapons? F*** that.
We can take you down with a Mars bar.'

00:17:33.700 --> 00:17:35.200
'Gentlemen, I have an announcement...

00:17:35.200 --> 00:17:37.680
'I have a Yorkie strategically positioned

00:17:37.680 --> 00:17:39.780
'over the major centres of the world.'

00:17:40.280 --> 00:17:42.280
Actually, yeah, that is your weapon of choice.

00:17:42.280 --> 00:17:44.500
Unless you can afford some of that posh dark chocolate.

00:17:44.500 --> 00:17:45.920
But I don't think the Swiss'd sell it to you.

00:17:46.080 --> 00:17:48.740
I don't think they'd do it. So yeah, basically the...

00:17:48.740 --> 00:17:51.600
The weapon of choice for Britain
in the space chocolate arms race

00:17:51.700 --> 00:17:53.620
— ...has to be the Yorkie.
— Has to be the Yorkie, yeah.

00:17:53.740 --> 00:17:56.000
I know the Swiss'd be wheeling out
the Toblerone and all that,

00:17:56.000 --> 00:17:57.880
but if you've got to keep up with the arms race,

00:17:57.880 --> 00:17:59.420
we'd just put up a fruit and nut one.

00:18:01.860 --> 00:18:03.860
[Laughter]

00:18:04.940 --> 00:18:07.400
Also I'm now just —
with you saying the fruit and nut,

00:18:07.400 --> 00:18:09.560
I'm now channeling the start of Flash Gordon.

00:18:09.760 --> 00:18:11.100
[Laughter]
TOM: Oh yeah.

00:18:11.240 --> 00:18:13.240
'Hot sultanas! Hot hazelnuts!'

00:18:13.360 --> 00:18:15.360
'Most effective, Your Majesty.'

00:18:15.480 --> 00:18:16.580
'Fudge sauce.'

00:18:18.880 --> 00:18:20.880
Just going around trying to catch it all with ice cream.

00:18:21.040 --> 00:18:22.660
[Laughter]

00:18:22.980 --> 00:18:25.420
'Sir, are you destroying the Earth or making a dessert?'

00:18:26.820 --> 00:18:27.460
Both.

00:18:27.600 --> 00:18:29.680
Little from Column A, little from Column B.

00:18:31.180 --> 00:18:33.540
'But how many will die as a result of this attack?'

00:18:33.920 --> 00:18:35.920
'Hundreds-and-thousands.'

00:18:39.000 --> 00:18:41.000
Yes! You win!

00:18:41.000 --> 00:18:43.180
Tom, he wins!

00:18:45.420 --> 00:18:48.980
I feel we've been building towards that moment
for the whole episode.

00:18:49.460 --> 00:18:52.460
Cut. Print. Let's go home.

00:18:52.700 --> 00:18:55.880
At the end of the show, Gary — congratulations, you win.

00:18:57.880 --> 00:18:59.660
Er, you've won...

00:19:02.660 --> 00:19:04.660
You've won a holiday to a French resort

00:19:04.660 --> 00:19:07.680
with Sulu from Star Trek
and the director of Transformers.

00:19:08.040 --> 00:19:10.360
It's George Takei and Michael Bay's
Week Away in Saint-Tropez.

00:19:11.720 --> 00:19:12.860
[Takei voice] 'Oh my.'

00:19:13.040 --> 00:19:13.880
Enjoy that!

00:19:13.880 --> 00:19:15.560
— That's been Matt Gray...
— Bye-bye!

00:19:15.560 --> 00:19:16.760
That's been Gary Brannan...

00:19:16.760 --> 00:19:18.020
That's been Chris Joel...

00:19:18.020 --> 00:19:19.860
I've been Tom Scott, and we'll see you next time.

00:19:23.860 --> 00:19:25.120
That was our show!

00:19:25.120 --> 00:19:27.120
If you liked it, give us a comment and let us know.

00:19:27.420 --> 00:19:29.680
And there are more than thirty audio episodes

00:19:29.680 --> 00:19:31.120
of our reverse trivia podcast

00:19:31.120 --> 00:19:33.760
over at techdif.co.uk.

